    "long_instructions": "Welcome back! In the last level, we learned that HTML is like a blueprint for building web pages. Now, let's start building our own blueprint for a web page about your pet fish!\r\n\r\n1. Look at the `index.html` file. This is where we'll write all our HTML code.\r\n2. You'll see some code already there. This is the very basic structure every HTML page needs. Let's break it down:\r\n    *   The first line, `<!DOCTYPE html>`, tells the browser that this is an HTML5 document.\r\n    *   Next, the `<html>` tag is like the main container for your entire web page. Everything else goes inside it.\r\n    *   Inside the `<html>` tag, you'll see two main sections: `<head>` and `<body>`.\r\n    *   The `<head>` section is for information *about* the page itself, like its title or links to stylesheets. This stuff usually isn't directly shown on the page.\r\n    *   The `<body>` section is where all the content that *does* show up on your web page goes – like text, images, and videos. Think of it as the main display area.\r\n\r\nYour task: Take a moment to read through the `index.html` file and make sure you understand where each of these main tags (`<html>`, `<head>`, `<body>`) are located. We'll be adding content to the `<body>` in the next level!",
    "generate_outline": "Guide students to create a new index.html file. Provide starter code for the basic HTML structure: <!DOCTYPE html>, <html>, <head>, and <body> tags. Instruct them to identify and understand the purpose of each tag."
  },
